A||||7||RobH||Reliability Factor - Auto vs. Manual||||5||
Z||000000||RobH||05-15-2000||08:43 AM||||I currently have an '85 5spd 4cyl SC Fiero, but have the opportunity to buy an '86 SE 6cyl Automatic w/ 173k miles for $1200.  I love my little 5spd but *hate* to change the clutch and it is about that time again...<P>Question is, which is more reliable, Auto or Manual?  What problems do the Auto's have compared to the Manuals?<P>The SE has power windows, sunroof, remote trunk opener, rear defroster, working A/C, but it idles high at about 1500-2000, and the owner said it burns a quart of oil between changes, but with that in mind, what do y'all think?<P>Thanks...<BR>Rob<P>||||reg||5
Z||000001||ChadMan||05-15-2000||09:06 AM||||To the best of my knowledge, the only chronic problem with the TH-125 automatic is a tendency for the torque converter lockup solenoid to fail.  Replacing it isn't easy but it's much easier than replacing a clutch!<P>$1200 sounds fair if the car looks good and hasn't been wrecked - but maybe he'd take $1000?||||reg||1
Z||000002||GT Bastard||05-15-2000||09:47 AM||||I've put lots of miles on two auto Fiero's and never had any problems aside from a cracked tranny cooler line.  Easy fix.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fformula88||05-15-2000||04:04 PM||||The Fiero auto's have been bullit-proof for my family.  I have 77K on my 88 Formula with no problems, and my dads 87 Coupe has 125K and still works perfectly.  No slipping.  The solenoid is still good (knock on wood!)||||reg||1
Z||000004||Homicidal||05-15-2000||04:41 PM||||My clutch went out, but besides that, no problem.  Clutches wear out, that's the problem with a manual.  <P>Autos I think are more reliable, but not by much.  Plus manual is SO much more fun to drive!||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||05-15-2000||09:51 PM||||Not in east coast traffic it's not. I'll never have another stick if I can avoid it around here.<P>Every clutch I've failed here has been the same thing... Pressure plate diaphram spring spilt. With the exception of owners who can't drive, all the ones I've replaced have been pressure plate problems as well. Only once, in up state N.Y. did I have one simply wear out the disk. Stop & go driving weakens the hell out of the plate spring long before the disk quits.<P>As far as raw reliability, an auto tranny that gets fresh fluid once in a while can last nearly forever. (Supposed to change it every 25-30k miles as recall.) The issue of TC solinoid failure is a minor one. It's time consuming to fix, not hard. No special tools are needed.<P>With the locking TC thats used in the TH125c milage diferances are fairly small between it and 4 speed. A 5 speed does a little better. (Top gear in the 3 & 4 speed sticks is usually straight thru. 5th gear is slightly over driven.)<P>uses oil? A quart? likely has a leak too. Depends where it is how big a deal it is to fix.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 05-15-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Old Lar||05-15-2000||11:00 PM||||I was out this weekend with someone who has an 86SE with over 200000 miles on it. Original engine , original t125 auto.  Must have been a car with everything all right.<P>How you drive it and maintain it makes the difference.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Standard||05-16-2000||03:53 AM||||The only major problem I know of with manuals(other than the clutch and hydraulics) is with the 3rd gear synchro in Getrags.  Mine's going out, and I think there are a few more forum members with the same problem.  If I double clutch it it slides in fine... ||||reg||1
